  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\n- Lingering showers possible through tonight\n\nThe main potential of rain (which in itself may be limited), will \nwrap up early this morning. This is as whatever line that can form \nwith low level jet dynamics on their way out and the short wave \nexiting the forecast area by 12z. \n\nA few showers will bubble up during the afternoon hours as we will \nhave the long wave trough over the area, with short waves rotating \nthrough. There may be just enough instability for a few rumbles of \nthunder. However the sufficient instability will be exiting the area \nby late afternoon.\n\nWe will not clear out right away, as we have some residual low level \nmoisture lingering around tonight into early Wednesday. Some of this \nis actually lake effect with lake temps in the upper teens to lower \n20s C and +7C air coming down. The trajectory is mainly from the NE, \nso it should stay west and east of the area off of Lakes Michigan \nand Huron.\n\n- Dry and cooler through Saturday, with next chance of rain Sunday\n\nWe should clear out nicely on Wednesday, with clouds, and by then \nsmoke also. We see a nice Canadian ridge build over the area, \nbringing much cooler temperatures and much drier air. \n\nWe see a large upper ridge take shape over the Plains, and we will \nbe downstream of that ridge under a confluent flow. This pattern \nwill lock in, and hold then likely through the first half of the \nweekend. We will start out cool, and gradually warm up as return \nflow brings warmer air in gradually, along with some additional \nmoisture.\n\nThere is actually decent agreement amongst the various models and \nensemble members that we see a strong enough short wave flatten the \nupper ridge temporarily, and then push a cold front into the area on \nSunday. This looks to be our next chance of rain after \ntoday/tonight.",
